.pdxdoc-version 1
.tool           libpdx-argv
.kind           library
.version        1.1.0
.author         paideia-os-team
.wave           R49
.summary        CLI argument parsing (long primary, short one-per-hyphen)
                for every R49+R50 P0 tool; also decodes the alternate
                semantic-pipe invocation record.

.section NAME
libpdx-argv - argv parser for PaideiaOS tools

.section SYNOPSIS
    #include this library in a tool's link line via `deps.list`
    then, at _start:

        flag_spec_reset()
        register_all()                                        // StdVocab
        flag_spec_register(<tool-flag-name>, <kind>, <id>)
        ...
        parsed_args_reset()
        let err = parse_argv(argv, argc)

    or, when invoked through a semantic pipe:

        let err = parse_from_schema_record(rec_ptr, rec_len)

    or, when the tool wants to accumulate every parse failure
    into one diagnostic pass (libpdx-argv.ENH-017, Closes #27):

        parsed_args_reset()
        let err = parse_argv_ex(argv, argc, ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS)
        for i in 0..error_count() {
            let (code, argv_i) = error_at(i)                  // rax:rdx
            // render one diagnostic line per (code, argv[argv_i])
        }
        // err = first-error code (same as pre-ENH-017 parse_argv
        // would have returned); ERR_OK iff every argv slot parsed
        // cleanly.

    ENH-030 (v1.1.0) renamed three previously-colliding globals:
    `ParsedArgs::reset`   -> parsed_args_reset
    `FlagSpec::reset`     -> flag_spec_reset
    `FlagSpec::register`  -> flag_spec_register
    `SchemaEmit::reset`   -> schema_emit_reset
    `SchemaEmit::register`-> schema_emit_register
    Pre-1.1 call sites that still use the bare names will not link.

.section DESCRIPTION
libpdx-argv turns a `_start`-time argv (or an equivalent
`PdxArgvRecord@0.1` semantic-pipe record) into a `ParsedArgs`
struct that a tool's dispatch loop reads by id. The library ships
no default caps and issues no syscalls of its own; every consumer
declares its own caps in its own `caps.decl`.

Two invocation paths converge on the same `ParsedArgs`:

  argv (textual)  --parse_argv-->  ParsedArgs
  wire (typed)    --parse_from_schema_record-->  ParsedArgs

The dispatch code that follows is invocation-path-agnostic; a
tool that renders `--help` via `doc <tool>` (see `HelpBackend`)
does so identically whether the user typed the flag or a peer
tool set the flag through a pipe.

.section FLAG GRAMMAR
The R49 grammar is fixed (see D3 in `design/tooling/plan.md`):

  --foo             boolean long flag
  --foo bar         long flag with lookahead value
  --foo=bar         long flag with inline value
  -x                single-letter short flag (one per hyphen)
  -abc              clustered short flags (libpdx-argv.ENH-013,
                     #23). Admitted IFF every letter's FlagSpec
                     registration is FKIND_BOOL — or, under
                     permissive mode, FKIND_UNKNOWN (unregistered,
                     treated as boolean per M2-001). Expanded to
                     N independent short-flag stores. A cluster
                     containing any value-consuming registration
                     fails with ERR_CLUSTER_WITH_ARITY (16). See
                     BREAKS WITH POSIX below.
  --                sentinel; all remaining tokens are positional
  -                 bare hyphen; REJECTED with ERR_UNKNOWN_ARG_FORM
                     (`libpdx-argv.ENH-026`). Tools that want the
                     stdin idiom must filter argv or register a
                     tool-specific stdin flag; the parser no longer
                     silently accepts `-` as a positional.
  ""                empty argv slot; REJECTED with ERR_UNKNOWN_ARG_FORM
                     (`libpdx-argv.ENH-026`).

.section BREAKS WITH POSIX
Clustered short flags are ADMITTED only when every letter is
FKIND_BOOL (or, under permissive mode, FKIND_UNKNOWN treated as
boolean per M2-001) — `libpdx-argv.ENH-013` (Closes #23). `-vv`
raises a verbosity counter twice; `-abc` where a/b/c are all
BOOL expands to three independent flag_count++ stores; the
`count_flag_by_id` accessor reports the repeat count naturally.
A cluster whose letters include ANY value-consuming
registration (STR/INT/TIMESPAN/SIZE/ENUM) is REJECTED with
`ERR_CLUSTER_WITH_ARITY` (16) BEFORE any letter is dispatched
(no partial storage; flag_count stays at what it was before the
cluster). Strict mode (`FlagSpec::set_strict(1)`) still rejects
an unregistered letter with `ERR_UNKNOWN_FLAG` (12).

The M1-002 D3 motivation survives: the specific hazard `-nX`
where `-n` is INT is still refused — the semantic pipe binds
flags by id and swallowing 'X' as the value would contradict
the cluster expansion `-n -X`. What ENH-013 relaxes is only
the mainstream `-vvv`/`-abc` idiom for BOOL/COUNTED short
flags, which composes with the existing `count_flag_by_id`
accessor without a new registration path.

The legacy `ERR_CLUSTERED_SHORT` (4) is now UNREACHABLE from
the parser but retained in `parsed_args.pdx` for wire-form
back-compat with any consumer that pattern-matched on it.

Long flags may accept a `=` separator OR a lookahead token, at
the tool's choice per `flag_spec_register` kind. The `:` separator
is reserved for `--no-cap:<name>` (I3-vocabulary) and for future
namespaced-flag forms; consumer tools may not repurpose it.

.section DUPLICATE FLAGS
Repeating a flag is never an error: every occurrence gets its own
storage slot and `flag_count` grows with each one. Three accessors
read the result (`libpdx-argv.ENH-008`):

  find_flag_by_id(id)       FIRST occurrence (default; unchanged
                             since M2)
  find_last_flag_by_id(id)  LAST occurrence — a later flag
                             overrides an earlier one, e.g.
                             `--color=auto --color=never` resolves
                             to `never`. This is the mainstream-CLI
                             convention and what makes shell
                             aliases composable (an alias that
                             appends `--color=never` wins over
                             whatever the user typed earlier).
  count_flag_by_id(id)      occurrence count, for flags where
                             repetition itself is the signal
                             (`-v -v -v` raising verbosity by 3).

A tool picks whichever accessor matches its own flag's semantics;
libpdx-argv does not force one policy on every flag.

.section STANDARD FLAGS (I3 vocabulary)
`StdVocab::register_all` binds these nine ids into `FlagSpec`
for every tool that calls it. IDs 1..9 are RESERVED for these
flags; a tool's own flags start at id 100.

  id 1  --help           back-end forwards to `doc <tool>` when
                          `doc` is statically linked (M3-002 default);
                          libpdx-argv.ENH-014 (#24) adds an in-process
                          fallback tools opt into via
                          `HelpBackend::set_doc_unavailable(1)` — see
                          the --HELP BACK-END and DIAGNOSTICS sections
                          below
  id 2  --version        library-owned auto-emitter (ENH-032, #25):
                          writes `<tool> <ver>\n<TOOL> VERSION OK\n`
                          to fd 1 and returns ERR_VERSION_EMITTED (13).
                          Opt out with VersionBackend::set_override(1).
  id 3  --dry-run        report what would change; don't do it
  id 4  --json           emit output as line-delimited JSON
  id 5  --schema         list this tool's declared output schemas
  id 6  --verbose        raise output verbosity by one step
  id 7  --quiet          suppress non-error output
  id 8  --color=<mode>   `auto`, `always`, `never`
  id 9  --no-cap:<name>  strip the named cap before exec (I7)

`--color` and `--no-cap` are registered separator-required
(`libpdx-argv.ENH-010`, `FlagSpec::register_sep`): the parser never
accepts `--color auto` or `--no-cap KIND_TTY` as a lookahead form —
only the inline spellings shown above. `--color file.txt` fails
`ERR_MISSING_VALUE` rather than swallowing `file.txt` as the colour
mode. A tool's own lookahead-permitted typed flags (registered via
the plain `flag_spec_register`) are unaffected.

.section TYPED FLAG ARGUMENTS
`Typed::parse_int_u64` / `parse_size` / `parse_timespan` decode
the string half of a typed flag into a `(ok: u64, value: u64)`
pair, returned directly in `rax:rdx`. These are leaf functions —
they never touch `ParsedArgs::error_code` at all; no
`ParsedArgs` constant exists for a typed-decode failure. A failing
decode (`ok == 0`) still leaves `flag_values[k]` pointing at the
offending text (the
parser stored that pointer regardless of what the value turns
out to decode to), so the tool renders its own diagnostic from
`ok` plus that pointer — this library does not do it for you.

  --older-than 7d       parse_timespan -> 604800 (seconds)
  --older-than 3h       parse_timespan -> 10800
  --size 2m             parse_size     -> 2097152
  --size 3g             parse_size     -> 3221225472
  -n 42                 parse_int_u64  -> 42

The `w` (week) suffix on `parse_timespan` is deferred to M6 (a
per-tool concern once anything ships a `--older-than 1w` need).

Overflow (`libpdx-argv.ENH-009`): all
three decoders detect a value that would exceed `u64::MAX` and
return `ok = 0` rather than silently wrapping (`--size 100g` no
longer turns into a small, wrong threshold; it fails). This applies
to the decimal-digit accumulation in all three, and to `parse_size`'s
suffix shift and `parse_timespan`'s suffix multiplier individually.

.section ALTERNATE INVOCATION (SEMANTIC PIPE)
When a tool is invoked through a `KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT` semantic
pipe with a `PdxArgvRecord@0.1` payload rather than through the
loader-seeded argv slot, the launcher hands the tool the
record's `(ptr, len)` in place of `(argv, argc)`. The tool
routes to `SchemaInvoke::parse_from_schema_record` instead of
`Parser::parse_argv`. Everything downstream — id-keyed dispatch,
typed-value decoding, `--help` forwarding — is identical.

The wire form is documented in `design/architecture.md` §10.1
and its schema fingerprint is registered with
`libpdx-semantic-pipe` M2 envelope framing.

Every offset the record carries (`name_off`, `value_off` when
present, `pos_off`) is bounds-checked against the record length
before it is dereferenced, and the resulting string is scanned
for a NUL terminator before the record ends — see DIAGNOSTICS
below. This is the library's only untrusted-input surface (a
peer process across the pipe), and no lookup is performed on a
pointer that fails either check.

.section --HELP BACK-END
`HelpBackend::fill_doc_argv` synthesises a two-slot argv of the
shape `["doc", tool_name]` in caller-provided storage and hands
it to the consumer, which then reaches `doc` through the shell.
libpdx-argv itself does not spawn `doc`; the consumer's own
loader-supplied cap chain decides whether the doc invocation is
permitted.

Auto-table fallback (`libpdx-argv.ENH-014`, #24). A tool built
WITHOUT `doc` statically linked has nowhere to route the M3-002
help pipe. At bootstrap the tool calls
`HelpBackend::set_doc_unavailable(1)` and populates each of its
registrations via `FlagSpec::register_with_help(name, kind, id,
help_ptr)` instead of the plain `flag_spec_register`; every
subsequent observation of `--help` (or a short flag registered
with id `STD_ID_HELP`) then triggers
`HelpBackend::emit_from_argspec` — a walk over the registration
table that writes one `--<name><TAB><help>\n` line to fd 1 per
registration whose help slot is non-null. Registrations whose
help slot is null are silently suppressed, so a tool that
populates ONLY some help slots gets ONLY those lines. The parser
returns `ERR_HELP_EMITTED` (15) via the same shared
`parse_argv_fail` epilogue every other `ERR_*` code takes; the
tool exits 0 in response.

The gate is opt-IN — default `doc_backend_unavailable == 0`
preserves the dispatch above (parse_argv stores `--help` as an
ordinary flag; the tool's own dispatch calls `fill_doc_argv` and
hands the pair to whichever `doc_dispatch` symbol it linked), so
every existing consumer sees zero behavior change on `--help`
without touching its bootstrap.

.section --SCHEMA
`schema_emit_register(schema_name_ptr)` at init lets a tool
publish which schemas it emits on stdout under normal operation.
`--schema` is ordinary `StdVocab` id 5 (see STANDARD FLAGS above)
— `parse_argv` stores it like any other registered flag;
it does not intercept it specially. The consumer's dispatch loop
checks `ParsedArgs::find_flag_by_id(StdVocab::STD_ID_SCHEMA)`,
and on a hit walks `SchemaEmit::get_count` / `SchemaEmit::get_name`
to print the declared schemas one per line, then exits 0. (This is
a different flag from the well-known `--pdx-schema` inline compare
described under ALTERNATE INVOCATION's `emit_schema` bit — `--schema`
lists declared output schemas; `--pdx-schema` is an M1 debug toggle
unrelated to SchemaEmit.)

Consumers that emit no schema-bearing output register nothing
(the flag is still accepted; the tool exits 0 with empty stdout).

.section CAPABILITY REQUIREMENTS
libpdx-argv itself requires none (see `caps.decl`). It reads
only the caller's own argv memory, and for the `--foo=bar` form
mutates that memory in place to null-terminate on `=`. It runs
synchronously inside the caller's process and, aside from the
`--version` auto-emitter and the `--help` auto-table fallback
documented below, issues no syscalls.

`HelpBackend::emit_from_argspec` (`libpdx-argv.ENH-014`, #24)
issues five `sys_write` syscalls on fd 1 per non-null row when
the parser observes `--help` and the tool has opted into the
fallback via `HelpBackend::set_doc_unavailable(1)`. The syscalls
inherit the caller's fd-1 cap; libpdx-argv holds no cap of its
own to gate them. Same silent-write policy as `emit_default`
above — a consumer that lacks a KIND_TTY / KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT cap
on fd 1 sees the syscalls fail with EBADF and the emitter
returns anyway; the tool exits after `parse_argv` returns
`ERR_HELP_EMITTED` (15) regardless.

`VersionBackend::emit_default` (`libpdx-argv.ENH-032`, #25) issues
seven raw `sys_write` syscalls on fd 1 when the parser observes
`--version` and the tool has not opted out via
`VersionBackend::set_override(1)`. The syscalls inherit the
caller's fd-1 cap; libpdx-argv holds no cap of its own to gate
them. Consumers that lack a KIND_TTY / KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT cap on
fd 1 see the syscalls fail with EBADF and the emitter returns
anyway — the auto-emit is best-effort, and the tool exits after
parse_argv returns ERR_VERSION_EMITTED (13) regardless.

.section DIAGNOSTICS
`Parser::parse_argv` and `SchemaInvoke::parse_from_schema_record`
both set `ParsedArgs::error_code`; `ERR_OK` (0) is success, and every
non-zero value is one of the constants below (all defined in
`src/parsed_args.pdx`; this list and that file's `pub let ERR_*` set
match exactly — libpdx-argv.ENH-005). Every non-zero code also sets
`ParsedArgs::error_arg_index` to the offending argv slot (argv path)
or flag/positional loop index (schema-record path); `0` for a
whole-buffer header failure.

  ERR_FLAG_OVERFLOW (1)             more than 32 flags in one parse
  ERR_POS_OVERFLOW (2)              more than 32 positionals in one parse
  ERR_CLUSTERED_SHORT (4)           (LEGACY) M1-002 clustered-short
                                     rejection. UNREACHABLE from
                                     Parser::parse_argv after
                                     libpdx-argv.ENH-013 (#23) relaxed
                                     the grammar. Constant retained in
                                     parsed_args.pdx for wire-form
                                     back-compat; see
                                     ERR_CLUSTER_WITH_ARITY (16) for
                                     the current cluster-reject code.
  ERR_LONG_MISSING_NAME (5)         `--=foo`, `--:foo`
  ERR_MISSING_VALUE (6)             typed flag reached end of argv with
                                     no value to consume, OR (ENH-010)
                                     a separator-required flag
                                     (--color, --no-cap) had no inline
                                     =/: value — its lookahead form is
                                     never accepted regardless of
                                     what follows in argv
  ERR_SCHEMA_BAD_MAGIC (7)          schema-record: bad wire magic
                                     (semantic-pipe path only)
  ERR_SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED_VERSION (8) schema-record: version != 1
                                     (semantic-pipe path only)
  ERR_SCHEMA_BAD_LAYOUT (9)         schema-record: header too short, a
                                     count exceeds MAX_FLAGS/MAX_POS, or
                                     the body-fits check failed
                                     (semantic-pipe path only)
  ERR_SCHEMA_BAD_OFFSET (10)        schema-record: name/value/pos
                                     offset out of range
                                     (semantic-pipe path only)
  ERR_SCHEMA_UNTERMINATED (11)      schema-record: string ran to record
                                     end with no NUL
                                     (semantic-pipe path only)
  ERR_UNKNOWN_FLAG (12)             unrecognised long/short name
                                     (opt-in: FlagSpec::set_strict(1);
                                     libpdx-argv.ENH-004. Permissive by
                                     default — an unregistered flag is
                                     silently accepted as boolean
                                     unless strict mode is enabled.)
  ERR_VERSION_EMITTED (13)          library-owned --version auto-emitter
                                     fired (libpdx-argv.ENH-032, #25).
                                     Not an error — a success signal
                                     the tool responds to with exit(0)
                                     (the fingerprint has already been
                                     written to fd 1). Opt out via
                                     VersionBackend::set_override(1) to
                                     render your own --version text.
  ERR_HELP_EMITTED (15)             library-owned --help auto-table
                                     fallback fired (libpdx-argv.ENH-014,
                                     #24). Not an error — a success
                                     signal the tool responds to with
                                     exit(0) (the table has already
                                     been written to fd 1). Fires only
                                     when the tool has opted into the
                                     fallback via
                                     HelpBackend::set_doc_unavailable(1);
                                     the default preserves the M3-002
                                     dispatch (parse_argv stores --help
                                     as ordinary flag). Klog tag:
                                     pdxargv.help-auto.

  ERR_CLUSTER_WITH_ARITY (16)       short-flag cluster `-abc` where at
                                     least one letter's FlagSpec::lookup
                                     returns a value-consuming kind
                                     (STR/INT/TIMESPAN/SIZE/ENUM). Set
                                     by libpdx-argv.ENH-013 (#23). No
                                     letter is dispatched; flag_count is
                                     unchanged from cluster entry (no
                                     partial storage). Strict mode's
                                     ENH-004 unregistered-name rule
                                     takes precedence when applicable:
                                     an unregistered letter under
                                     set_strict(1) fires ERR_UNKNOWN_FLAG
                                     (12) instead. Klog tag:
                                     pdxargv.short-cluster.

  ERR_BAD_INT (17)                  FKIND_INT flag's value failed
                                     Typed::parse_int_u64 decoding
                                     (libpdx-argv.ENH-017, #27). Fires
                                     ONLY when the caller passed
                                     ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS in
                                     parse_argv_ex's parse_flags; the
                                     pre-ENH-017 parse_argv (and
                                     parse_argv_ex with parse_flags=0)
                                     never validates INT values at
                                     parse time — the consumer's own
                                     dispatch-site decoder catches
                                     them exactly as before. When
                                     fired, the stored flag slot is
                                     NOT rolled back: flag_names[k] /
                                     flag_values[k] / flag_ids[k] /
                                     flag_kinds[k] all reflect the
                                     (name, raw-string-value, id,
                                     INT) triple the parser observed.
                                     argv_index attaches to the value
                                     slot (not the flag slot), so a
                                     consumer's diagnostic renderer
                                     dereferences argv[argv_index] to
                                     get the failing token verbatim.
                                     Not emitted on the SchemaInvoke
                                     path (typed values are decoded
                                     upstream). Klog tag:
                                     pdxargv.collect-errors.

  ERR_STRING_ENUM (18)              FKIND_STR flag's captured value
                                     did not byte-equal (case-
                                     sensitive) any entry in the
                                     caller-registered allowed set
                                     (libpdx-argv.ENH-019, #29). Set
                                     by Parser::parse_argv on both
                                     the long-flag (`--color auto`
                                     and `--color=auto`) and single-
                                     letter short-flag store paths
                                     immediately after the store
                                     block, when the just-stored
                                     kind is FKIND_STR (1) AND
                                     FlagSpec::last_lookup_allowed_count
                                     (published by the preceding
                                     call lookup) is nonzero AND no
                                     candidate in the allowed_ptr
                                     array matches. The check fires
                                     on BOTH parse_argv and
                                     parse_argv_ex (independent of
                                     the ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS
                                     bit) because the allowed set
                                     is a per-registration property
                                     opted into at
                                     FlagSpec::register_string_enum
                                     time — the caller has
                                     explicitly asked for the gate.
                                     Empty allowed_count is the
                                     "gate OFF" sentinel: a plain
                                     flag_spec_register(name,
                                     FKIND_STR, id) writes 0 into
                                     spec_allowed_count via the
                                     defensive-zero block, so
                                     pre-ENH-019 STR registrations
                                     preserve byte-for-byte
                                     behaviour. Case-sensitive: no
                                     ASCII-case normalisation is
                                     applied anywhere in the walk
                                     (`"AUTO"` never matches an
                                     `"auto"` entry) — callers
                                     wanting case-insensitive
                                     vocabularies register the
                                     equivalence classes explicitly.
                                     The stored flag slot is NOT
                                     rolled back — the parser-
                                     observed (name, offending-value,
                                     id, STR) triple stays in
                                     flag_names[k] / flag_values[k]
                                     / flag_ids[k] / flag_kinds[k]
                                     for a consumer's diagnostic
                                     rendering. Cluster path
                                     (parse_argv_short_clustered)
                                     is BOOL-only per ENH-013's
                                     admissibility rule, so no enum
                                     walk fires there. Klog tag:
                                     pdxargv.string-enum.

  ERR_INT_RANGE (14)                INT-flag value fell outside the
                                     registered inclusive-unsigned
                                     [min, max] interval
                                     (libpdx-argv.ENH-018, #28). Set by
                                     Typed::parse_int_u64_ranged only —
                                     Parser::parse_argv never writes
                                     this code; it fires at the
                                     dispatch-site decoder call.
                                     Sentinel (min = 0 AND max = 0)
                                     means "range OFF" and never trips
                                     it; the plain
                                     flag_spec_register(name,
                                     FKIND_INT, id) path writes
                                     exactly this, so every existing
                                     INT registration is transparent
                                     to the gate. A tool wanting an
                                     interval registers via
                                     FlagSpec::register_int(name, id,
                                     min, max) and calls
                                     Typed::parse_int_u64_ranged
                                     (with its own bounds or
                                     get_range_by_id-recovered ones)
                                     at the dispatch site. Klog tag:
                                     pdxargv.int-range.

`ERR_UNKNOWN_ARG_FORM` (3) fires when an argv slot is neither
flag-shaped (starts with `-` AND has at least one byte after) NOR a
valid positional (does NOT start with `-` AND is non-empty)
(`libpdx-argv.ENH-026`). Concrete triggers today: an empty string
`""` argv slot, and bare `-`. Bare `--` remains the M2-003
positional sentinel (unchanged) and does not fire this code. Slots
that appear after the `--` sentinel are always positional and never
reach the malformed classifier. Typed-decode failures
(`Typed::parse_*` returning `ok == 0`) do not set `error_code` at
all — see TYPED FLAG ARGUMENTS above — and the schema-record path's
only failure codes are the four listed above.

This library does not itself call `exit` or prescribe a process exit
status for any code above — mapping `ParsedArgs::error_code` to a
concrete exit number (and rendering the human-readable diagnostic on
stderr) is entirely the consumer's own I4 exit-code-discipline
obligation (`design/tooling/plan.md` T-INV-I4). Do not treat any
exit-status split by "code group" as a contract this library defines.

.section OUTPUT SCHEMAS
libpdx-argv declares one output wire schema
(libpdx-argv.ENH-016, Closes #26):

  PdxArgvParseErrorRecord@0.1  emitted by SchemaEmit::emit_parse_error
                                on any parse failure. See src/parse_error_record.pdx
                                for the canonical layout definition.

Wire form (v1). Total record = ((32 + token_len + 7) / 8) * 8
bytes; header is fixed at 32 bytes; tail is zero-padded to the
next 8-byte boundary so a consumer streaming multiple records
back-to-back never has to re-align.

  | Offset | Size | Field       |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  |  0 | 8 | magic `"PDXAPERR"` ASCII (bytes 0x50 0x44 0x58 0x41 0x50 0x45 0x52 0x52) |
  |  8 | 8 | version (u64 LE; must equal 1)                                          |
  | 16 | 4 | err_code   (u32 LE; the `ParsedArgs::ERR_*` code)                      |
  | 20 | 4 | argv_index (u32 LE; `ParsedArgs::error_arg_index`)                     |
  | 24 | 4 | token_len  (u32 LE; token byte count, excluding any NUL)               |
  | 28 | 4 | token_off  (u32 LE; always 32 in v1)                                   |
  | 32 | token_len | token bytes (verbatim copy of the offending argv slot; no NUL) |
  | next | 0..7 | zero padding to the next 8-byte boundary                          |

Semantics.

  - err_code carries the same non-zero ERR_* code parse_argv or
    parse_from_schema_record returned in rax and stored in
    ParsedArgs::error_code — never zero (emit_parse_error returns
    0 without writing on error_code == 0). u32 truncation of the
    u64 slot is safe: every ERR_* value fits in a byte today
    (max 15) and the reserved u32 space covers every plausible
    post-1.2 addition.
  - argv_index is ParsedArgs::error_arg_index verbatim — the
    offending argv slot on the argv path, or the flag/pos loop
    index on the SchemaInvoke path (0 for whole-header failures
    on either path).
  - token_off is the offset of the token region WITHIN THE
    RECORD (relative to buf, not argv). It is always 32 in v1;
    an explicit field rather than an implicit constant so a v2
    header extension can grow past 32 bytes without a wire re-cut.
  - token_len bytes of the offending argv slot's text follow at
    offset 32, verbatim from the caller's argv, WITHOUT a NUL
    terminator. The source argv slot IS NUL-terminated (every
    argv slot is), but the record does not store the NUL — a
    downstream reader that needs a C-string derefs token_off,
    reads token_len bytes, and appends its own NUL.

Bounds discipline. `emit_parse_error(buf, buflen, argv_ptr)`
computes the padded record size and refuses to write anything
when `buflen < padded` (the record is atomic per invocation —
no partial writes). Callers reserving a scratch buffer for the
emit should size it against the tool's longest plausible argv
slot; 128 bytes covers every I3 flag spelling plus a room-to-
grow margin (the longest today is `--no-cap:KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT` at
25 bytes). A caller that supplies a nonzero `argv_ptr` gets the
token bytes populated; passing `argv_ptr == 0` (typical on the
SchemaInvoke path, where there is no argv to point at) still
emits the record with `token_len = 0` and the header carries
`error_arg_index` intact.

The consumer wiring pattern:

    parsed_args_reset()
    let err = parse_argv(argv, argc)
    if err != ERR_OK {
        let mut errbuf : [u8; 128] = uninit @align(8)
        let n = SchemaEmit::emit_parse_error(&errbuf, 128, argv)
        // n == padded record size, or 0 on buflen too small
        // ...consumer forwards n bytes to its audit/pipe consumer...
    }

--schema surface. The schema name is published to
`SchemaEmit::schema_emit_register` from the consumer's bootstrap
(the tool owns which of libpdx-argv's declared schemas it
exposes on `--schema`):

    SchemaEmit::schema_emit_register(&ParseErrorRecord::PERR_SCHEMA_NAME_V01)

The 28-byte `PERR_SCHEMA_NAME_V01` is `"PdxArgvParseErrorRecord@0.1\0"`
in .rodata (27 chars + NUL).

