.section HISTORY
  1.0.0  2026-08-22   First signed release. Closes M1..M5.
  Unreleased          libpdx-argv.ENH-014 (Closes #24) — auto --help
                       table fallback. Adds
                       FlagSpec::register_with_help + spec_help slot,
                       HelpBackend::set_doc_unavailable /
                       emit_from_argspec / pdxargv_help_reset, and
                       ParsedArgs::ERR_HELP_EMITTED (15). A tool built
                       without `doc` opts in via set_doc_unavailable(1)
                       and populates help slots via register_with_help;
                       parse_argv on --help then walks FlagSpec and
                       prints --<name><TAB><help>\n per non-null row.
                       Default preserves M3-002 dispatch — existing
                       consumers see zero behavior change.
  Unreleased          libpdx-argv.ENH-018 (Closes #28) — INT flag
                       range validation. Adds FlagSpec::register_int
                       and get_range_by_id, Typed::parse_int_u64_ranged,
                       and ParsedArgs::ERR_INT_RANGE (14). A tool
                       registering `--jobs` with (1, 64) sees
                       `--jobs 32` decode OK, `--jobs 0` / `--jobs 65`
                       / `--jobs UINT64_MAX` all rejected with
                       ERR_INT_RANGE. Plain register keeps the
                       range OFF (0, 0 sentinel).
  Unreleased          libpdx-argv.ENH-019 (Closes #29) — STRING
                       flag enum validation. Adds
                       FlagSpec::register_string_enum (kind fixed
                       to FKIND_STR, publishes an *const *const u8
                       array-of-NUL-terminated-string pointers +
                       count into new spec_allowed_ptr /
                       spec_allowed_count [u64; 32] slots), a
                       lookup() companion publish
                       (last_lookup_allowed_ptr /
                       last_lookup_allowed_count) so
                       Parser::parse_argv_ex can byte-compare (case-
                       sensitive) the just-stored value against
                       every allowed entry via an inline
                       xor+mov_b+cmp strcmp walk, and
                       ParsedArgs::ERR_STRING_ENUM (18). A tool
                       registering `--color` with {auto, always,
                       never} sees `--color auto`/`--color always`/
                       `--color never` OK, `--color mauve`/`--color
                       AUTO` rejected. Empty allowed_count = gate
                       OFF; plain flag_spec_register(name, FKIND_STR,
                       id) preserves pre-ENH-019 behaviour. Gate
                       fires on BOTH parse_argv and parse_argv_ex
                       (independent of ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS) —
                       the allowed set is a per-registration opt-in.
                       Store NOT rolled back on rejection (matches
                       ERR_BAD_INT discipline).
  Unreleased          libpdx-argv.ENH-017 (Closes #27) — collect-all-
                       errors mode. Adds Parser::parse_argv_ex(argv,
                       argc, parse_flags) as the primary entry point;
                       existing parse_argv becomes a thin wrapper
                       passing parse_flags=0 (byte-for-byte backward
                       compatible). Bit 0 of parse_flags
                       (ARGV_COLLECT_ALL_ERRORS = 1) opts into: (a)
                       keep parsing after every recoverable failure,
                       accumulating up to MAX_COLLECTED_ERRORS (16)
                       records into ParsedArgs::error_ring_codes /
                       error_ring_indices (16B per slot × 16 slots
                       = 256B .bss); (b) inline Typed::parse_int_u64
                       validation of every FKIND_INT flag's value,
                       recording ERR_BAD_INT (17) on decode failure.
                       New readers ParsedArgs::error_count and
                       error_at (multi-return rax:rdx pair). First-
                       error semantics preserved on every exit
                       (parse_argv_epilogue reads error_ring_codes[0]
                       into rax for the return). Overflow (ERR_
                       FLAG_OVERFLOW / ERR_POS_OVERFLOW) still stops
                       the parse under both modes; ring caps silently
                       (extra errors dropped from the ring, parse
                       still advances).

